AD  - SRM Institute of Science and Technology, Faculty of Engineering and Technology, Kattankulathur, India
AB  - The rapid expansion of cloud service providers has challenges such as privacy preservation and dynamic resource provisioning that occur due to its heterogeneity. The multiple users in the cloud computing environment lead to the delay in request and service selection. The traditional methods have some constraints which include trust management issues, service interruptions, security issues, and access control loss that lead to performance degradation. The existing resource allocation approaches often rely on one-dimensional outputs, which lead to inaccurate predictions and limited information, particularly when handling multi-dimensional and intricate resource requirements. Hence, this paper proposes a novel transformer-based approach named as American Zebra Optimized Fuzzy Multivariate Residual Gated Attention Transformer model for dynamic resource provisioning in a cloud computing environment. The GWA-T-12 Bit brains dataset is used in this paper, the tasks are gathered from the different users present in the cloud environment. The collected tasks are prioritized based on the diverse factors, including task size, latency sensitivity, deadline sensitivity, and network bandwidth. The Fuzzy logic based Priority Scheduler prioritizes the collected tasks that are grouped into three like Group P1, Group P2, and Group P3. The Multivariate Residual Gated Attention Transformer is implemented to forecast multivariate time-series data. For task scheduling, the American Zebra Optimization Algorithm is implemented for trust-aware multi-objective task scheduling. The time-series foresting and task scheduling experiments are conducted in this paper with diverse evaluation measures and comprehensive analysis. The results demonstrated that the proposed model surpassed the traditional techniques and achieved a higher success rate of 97.5%, turnaround efficiency of 98.3%, VM availability rate of 95.8%, and lower makespan of 852 ms, load balance of 1.526, and RMSE of 6.573 respectively. © 2025 Elsevier B.V., All rights reserved.
