UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-105000270721&doi=10.1145%2F3704137.3704188&partnerID=40&md5=544d79fee8f4d6e80d15e03a5e5194d9
AD  - University of the Ryukyus, Information Technology Center, Nishihara, Japan
AB  - Edge cloud computing is rapidly gaining traction across various domains, including data analysis. However, concerns regarding privacy breaches due to accidental data leakage have been raised. To address this issue, we propose a privacy-preserving Bayesian inference method specifically designed for sparse Gaussian processes. The proposed method enables Bayesian inference for sparse Gaussian processes while keeping the input data, including inducing points, scrambled through a random unitary transform (RUT). We prove that our Bayesian inference for scrambled data performs the same as Bayesian inference for non-scrambled data. Furthermore, we propose an access control method for inference by changing the private keys of the RUT for training and test data. Finally, we demonstrate the effectiveness of our method through experiments with diabetes data from the medical analysis field. © 2025 Elsevier B.V., All rights reserved.
