TI  - Optimizing Operational Processes and Reducing Equipment Failure Through a Real Time Alerting Platform
PY  - 2025
VL  - 2025-March
DO  - 10.2118/223809-MS
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-86000200579&doi=10.2118%2F223809-MS&partnerID=40&md5=376983d5929787748ea5879b0a118aed
AD  - Patterson UTI, Houston, United States
AD  - Accenture Services Pvt Ltd., India, Bengaluru, India
AB  - The ability to monitor data in real time has become increasingly important as the industry continues digitalization efforts. Over the past several years this contractor has upgraded the capabilities of a real time monitoring system that has been providing value through improved operational efficiency and visibility of operations across its onshore fleet. These updates open a wide set of alerting opportunities that were previously not possible by distributing the alerting system to local, rig-based devices. An overview of the technology and architecture necessary to make this system possible, as well as the impact on performance will be discussed. This contractor's efforts have focused on converting the system from a cloud-based system to an edge-based system, with all computations and alerts issued at the rig location. The architecture comprises migrating cloud applications to run on the edge and leveraging edge hardware and rig historians to enable alerting on all data available at the rig location. Streamlining and synchronizing the rules/alert definitions and notifying users instantly using cloud infrastructure is a novel implementation. Housed within the edge platform, the system can operate on batches of data in real time without being held back by cloud computing related latency. Preexisting infrastructure made it possible to receive exception-based alerting within 15 minutes of an event occurring. With the new implementation running on the edge, it is possible to receive alerts to the user's phone, email and/or web-based dashboard within a minute. Leveraging increased real-time monitoring capabilities, this contractor has been able to reduce the amount of premature equipment failures as well as improve drilling connection operations. These successes have resulted in a reduction in capital spend and improved spud to release times. The above will be reviewed in detail. This change in data architecture from a cloud-based to a distributed rig-based computing system provides a more efficient way to trigger alerts and opens the possibility of generating alerts locally at the driller's console. Previously, the cloud-based alerting system only had access to data streamed off-location, providing limited insight into equipment health and drilling conditions. As a result of this update, we can provide end users with alert notifications containing detailed messages and specific data from a wide range of local data sources, including thousands of equipment-related data feeds, downhole data, complex artificial intelligence(AI)/machine learning(ML) model outputs, and drilling performance metrics which were not previously synchronized into a system that allowed alerts to be generated across this entire dataset. Quicker computing and notification delivery times will pave the way for well control, drilling dysfunction and more sophisticated equipment health alerts on the driller's console in real time. © 2025 Elsevier B.V., All rights reserved.
