AD  - Amity University, Noida, India
AD  - Indian Institute of Information Technology, Allahabad, Prayagraj, India
AB  - Trust is the most important factor, which was a critical deciding factor in all security measures of traditional perimeter security models. However, the dynamic nature of attacks has changed the scenario drastically, where these models failed to address the failures. VPNs, firewalls, DMZs, and other identity-based access models—all security controls—were a good fit for protecting contemporary legacy devices and classical models. However, evolving AI and generative models of AI have pushed these security models to their limitations. In this book chapter, the limitations of these legacy devices have been discussed. A comparative analysis of all devices, comprising VPN, firewall, IAM, DMZ, and so forth, has been conducted, focusing on their demerit cases considering trust as a factor, inside or outside. This chapter has also examined how the advancement of cloud computing, hybrid networks, and complex IT environments, including mobile frameworks, is giving rise to a situation in which the existing model is inadequate. Additionally, it has explored how the zero-trust security framework can offer its assistance in this context. © 2025 Elsevier B.V., All rights reserved.
