PY  - 2025
SP  - 76
EP  - 83
DO  - 10.1109/ICSC65596.2025.11140185
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-105016659285&doi=10.1109%2FICSC65596.2025.11140185&partnerID=40&md5=450c6111eed6b5bb6a037ee6bb7a9d82
AD  - University of New Haven, Tagliatela College of Engineering, West Haven, United States
AB  - Traditional authentication systems, reliant on single-checkpoint verification, remain vulnerable to post-authentication threats such as impersonation and session hijacking. This paper introduces a novel continuous authentication framework that leverages gait biometrics—a natural, spoof-resistant modality—enhanced by machine learning (ML) and real-time 3D skeletal data capture. Using a Microsoft Kinect sensor, kinematic features (e.g., stride length, joint angles) are extracted and processed via a Random Forest classifier to authenticate users dynamically. The system demonstrates 52.17% accuracy in controlled environments, though performance declines by 5% under suboptimal conditions (e.g., low lighting, obstacles). A modular microservices architecture ensures scalability, while encryption techniques and GDPR-compliant protocols preserve privacy. Challenges in heterogeneous environments underscore the need for multi-sensor fusion and deep learning integration. By balancing security and usability, this work advances gait biometrics as a passive, resilient solution for high-stakes applications such as secure access control, offering a critical step toward real-world deployment of continuous authentication systems. © 2025 Elsevier B.V., All rights reserved.
