AD  - Columbia Business School, New York, United States
AB  - Attribute-Based Access Control (ABAC) provides a robust and flexible security framework that facilitates precise, context-aware access control by evaluating various dynamic attributes related to users, resources, and environmental factors. Their applications are used in microservices and offer a scalable solution for securing distributed systems, addressing the limitations of earlier role-based access control in managing complex and multi-dimensional access policies. This paper discusses developing and deploying an ABAC model designed explicitly for microservices, incorporating policy engines, attribute-driven rules, and contextual parameters such as time, location, and user behavior to make accurate access control decisions. Integrating ABAC into a microservices architecture enables organizations to enhance security, simplify policy management, and maintain compliance with changing regulatory standards, all while preserving performance and scalability. © 2025 Elsevier B.V., All rights reserved.
