TI  - Port Forwarding Services Are Forwarding Security Risks
PY  - 2025
SP  - 289
EP  - 303
DO  - 10.1109/EuroSP63326.2025.00025
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-105016255515&doi=10.1109%2FEuroSP63326.2025.00025&partnerID=40&md5=8b49f72dab26b51096a8465692d9222b
AD  - University of Science and Technology of China, Hefei, China
AD  - Microsoft Research, Redmond, United States
AB  - We conduct the first comprehensive security study on two widely used port forwarding services (PFS), which emerge in recent years and make the web services deployed in internal networks available on the Internet along with better usability but less complexity compared to traditional techniques (e.g., NAT traversal techniques). Our study is made possible through a set of effective and efficient methodologies, which are designed to uncover the technical mechanisms of PFS, experiment attack scenarios for PFS protocols, automatically discover and snapshot port-forwarded websites (PFWs) at scale, and classify PFWs into well-observed categories. Leveraging these methodologies, we have observed the widespread adoption of PFS with millions of PFWs distributed across tens of thousands of ISPs worldwide. Furthermore, 32.31% PFWs have been classified into website categories that serve access to critical infrastructure or data, such as, web consoles for industrial control systems, IoT controllers, code repositories, and office automation systems. And 18.57% PFWs did not enforce any access control for external visitors. Also identified are two types of attacks inherent in the protocols of Oray (one well-adopted PFS provider), and the notable abuse of PFSes by malicious actors in activities such as malware distribution, botnet operation and phishing. © 2025 Elsevier B.V., All rights reserved.
