AD  - Arab American University, Palestine, Department of Natural, Jenin, Palestine
AB  - As the threats in cyber space are increasingly becoming sophisticated, traditional security models are found to be insufficient to protect critical systems and therefore the shift towards resilient models such as the Zero Trust Model is inevitable. It evaluates if the Zero Trust Model is effective in avoiding cyber-attacks in a mixed-method study by incorporating qualitative results among IT professionals supported by quantitative tests in a simulated virtual environment. The experiment tried three security models: an open system, a system defended with a traditional firewall, and a system defended with the Zero Trust Model. Security performance metrics - attack success rate, lost information, and overall resilience - were compared by attempting to hack the system using simulated SYN Flood and hybrid cyber-attacks. Results showed that the Zero Trust Model successfully repelled 100% of malicious packets, whereas traditional firewalls remained vulnerable. Interestingly, the network secured by the firewall received 1.93 times more packets than the Zero Trust-secured network that was attacked. An electronic bank withdrawal case study was also performed to assess Zero Trust as far as securing online transactions is concerned. Banks implementing the Zero Trust system were successful in blocking unauthorized withdrawals, while their counterparts lost funds due to violations. The findings reveal that the widespread long-term adoption of the Zero Trust Model leads to the drastic reduction of security breaches and enhances access control and overall cybersecurity posture. These findings are underpinned by evidence-based proposals for enhancing cybersecurity measures against the evolving threat landscape targeting organizational critical systems. © 2025 Elsevier B.V., All rights reserved.
