AD  - Metro State University, Department of Computer Science and Cybersecurity, Saint Paul, United States
AD  - Department of Computer and Information Sciences, Philadelphia, United States
AB  - Organizations are increasingly adopting multi-cloud strategies, leveraging the distinct capabilities provided by vendors such as Amazon Web Services (AWS), Microsoft Azure, and Google Cloud Platform (GCP). Nonetheless, the unique methodologies of each platform for network access control present novel challenges in configuration, validation, and enforcement. Current studies on firewall rule misconfiguration generally presume a singular device or a single-cloud framework, neglecting the intricacies of multi-cloud settings. This study analyzes the disparity between conventional firewall assumptions and the contemporary diverse cloud environment. We demonstrate through practical examples how a configuration that is secure in one cloud can represent a vulnerability in another in a multi-cloud environment. We provide a systematic comparison of AWS, Azure, and GCP firewall architectures, emphasizing significant distinctions in rule semantics, assessment logic, and attachment points. We offer a cohesive architectural framework that standardizes provider-specific regulations, perpetually identifies deviations, and validates network access in accordance with planned standards. Our work aims to assist practitioners in managing intricate multi-cloud deployments with enhanced confidence and consistency by identifying the shortcomings of existing methodologies and delineating a strategy for scalable, cross-platform solutions. © 2025 Elsevier B.V., All rights reserved.
