AB  - The unlabeled data that represents most of the acquired dataset poses the most difficult problem in the process of creating a classification model for risk prediction during COVID-19. Those unlabeled datasets characterize the individuals in health exams, for whom the health states can range from extremely healthy to severely ill, depending on the assessment. There is no objective way to distinguish between their varied states of health, surgical procedures, lab tests, observations, diagnostic reports, prescriptions, patient data, and allergies are all examples of information that can be found in an electronic health record (EHR). A personal health record (PHR), often known as a PHR, is a newly emerging patient-centered model for the interchange of health information. The storage of PHRs is typically outsourced to a third party, such as a cloud storage provider. Encrypting patients’ health information before transferring them to a third party for processing is one technique that could help consumers retain control over who gets access to their own PHRs. This chapter introduces a novel paradigm called the patient-centric paradigm as well as a set of strategies for controlling data access to PHRs stored on semi-trusted servers. Achieve greater attention to data access control that is also scalable for PHRs, each patient’s health record file is encrypted utilizing attribute-based encryption (ABE) technologies. Using the multi-authority technique protects a substantial degree of patient confidentiality at the same time. Furthermore, this system enables the dynamic change of access restrictions or file attributes, supports efficient user revocation, and gives access when required. © 2025 Elsevier B.V., All rights reserved.
