AD  - Saranathan College of Engineering, Thiruchirappalli, Department of Electronics and Communication Engineering, Tiruchirappalli, India
AB  - The overview of cybersecurity in intelligent and sustainable manufacturing (ISM) aims to provide insight into the risks and threats facing manufacturing systems and processes, along with the strategies to ensure their security and protection. Integrating advanced technologies like cloud computing, artificial intelligence (AI), and the Internet of Things (IoT) has enhanced productivity and sustainability in manufacturing. However, this integration also introduces new security challenges, making systems susceptible to cyberattacks that can compromise data, disrupt operations, and lead to financial losses. Establishing a robust cybersecurity architecture is essential to safeguard production systems. This involves implementing measures such as encryption, firewalls, and access controls, conducting regular security assessments and audits, and having a clear incident response strategy. Continuously analyzing and upgrading cybersecurity measures is vital to staying ahead of emerging threats. Collaboration among manufacturers, technology providers, and government agencies is crucial to ensure the security and reliability of manufacturing systems in the era of ISM. As technology advances rapidly, securing networks and systems from cyberattacks has become paramount for manufacturers to avoid data loss, production disruptions, and financial harm. Adopting a comprehensive cybersecurity approach encompassing technology and best practices is imperative to address these concerns effectively. © 2025 Elsevier B.V., All rights reserved.
