AD  - K L Deemed to be University, Department of Computer Science and Engineering, Vaddeswaram, India
AB  - The study’s major focus is on protecting confidential patient information, as well as improving cloud-based health-care security. The report examines how health-care data management is currently managed, emphasizing the need to transition to cloud-based infrastructures and weighing the pros and cons of doing so. It studies emerging cybersecurity threats in the medical industry and highlights vulnerabilities that could lead to patient data breaches. The report recommends a comprehensive solution that includes better encryption, safe access limitations, and cutting-edge anomaly detection technologies. To stay ahead of new cyberthreats, it investigates how artificial intelligence and machine learning might be used to predict and prevent security breaches. The article includes case studies and real-world examples to validate the technique and demonstrate the effectiveness of heightened security measures in reducing risks and protecting patient data. The findings help health-care firms improve their security posture in cloud environments by providing best practices. In addition to offering a comprehensive examination of the challenges at the intersection of cloud computing and health care, the report concludes with a strategic strategy for improving patient data protection. Health-care businesses can protect patient confidentiality, regulatory compliance, and availability by implementing recommended security measures in an increasingly digital health-care environment. © 2024 Elsevier B.V., All rights reserved.
