AD  - Indrashil University, Department of Computer Science and Engineering, Mehsana, India
AB  - From small businesses to major corporations, Cloud computing is quickly taking over as the preferred platform. due to the obligation to guarantee that cloud apps are safe and can shield data from unauthorized attackers. According to the joint responsibility model of Cloud Security, organizations who utilize these services are also responsible for maintaining their degree of security while service providers are responsible for making sure that their services are safe. A shared responsibility Paradigm for identity and access management transfers part of the client's responsibility for application security. Setting up procedures for identity management and access control as well as carrying out ethical hacking and penetration testing are required to guarantee the greatest degree of data security. Since Cloud Security has developed into a shared duty between the user and the service provider, clients are now required to take accountability for the security of their applications in the cloud. IaaS and PaaS services are the major beneficiaries of this. Advanced security procedures like ethical hacking and penetration testing are necessary to provide a safe virtual environment. Organizations will be able to avoid harmful assaults and data leaks thanks to these efforts. Raising risk visibility, identifying vulnerabilities, minimizing risks, and gathering crucial telemetry data are the greatest ways to enhance security. The primary goal of this study article is to assess the techniques for designing a framework or checklist to identify associated risks & vulnerabilities while at the same time attacking cloud-based applications. As a consequence, malicious actors will be kept out of cloud-based programs. © 2024 Elsevier B.V., All rights reserved.
