AB  - Internet of Things (IoT) security encompasses the protection of various components including devices, cloud infrastructure, software, mobile applications, network interfaces, encryption methods, authentication protocols, and physical security measures. The vulnerabilities in IoT application data security, outlined by the Open Web Application Security Project (OWASP), stem from issues like insecure web interfaces, inadequate authentication/authorization, flawed software/firmware, privacy apprehensions, insecure network services, absence of transport encryption, compromised cloud interfaces, vulnerable mobile interfaces, limited security configurability, weak physical security, and both application and endpoint security. Cellular IoT confronts a range of signaling attacks, including threats to identification, location privacy breaches, data interception, tapping, and denial of service. In the context of 5G-IoT, the sudden surge in interconnected mesh networks facilitated by IoT devices introduces critical security challenges. The conventional hub-and-spoke model has been fundamentally transformed with the incorporation of edge computing techniques. In this meshed environment, any weak point can potentially become a security vulnerability, heightening the importance of robust security measures in the 5G- and 6G-enabled IoT landscape. The chapter primarily centers around in-depth discussions concerning these security issues and provides insights into potential avenues for future research aimed at mitigating these challenges. © 2024 Elsevier B.V., All rights reserved.
