AD  - American International University - Bangladesh, Department of Computer Science, Dhaka, Bangladesh
AB  - E-banking is one of the most indispensable parts of banking and money transactions in an increasingly digitalized world. With truly global economies as well as wide-scale digitalization of services, e-banking is a key sector for development as well as advancement due to changing technological and security constraints which not only help create new transaction and user experiences but also create new interaction methods, use cases, and security domains. These aspects are prominent in countries with developing economies and banking sectors, such as Bangladesh. Alongside the country's growing economy, its e-banking sector is also rapidly developing to adapt to new technological advances and cater to the local market. With these new growth paradigms, questions of security and protection are commonplace. Historically, the country's banking sector has been subject to various cyber security attacks on both the consumer and enterprise side. While some of these are affected by technological vulnerabilities, others are due to fraud and extortion. With digitalization and e-banking, the country's banking sector has been able to mitigate various cyber security issues, while new cyber security threats have become prominent due various infrastructural or internal issues. This research aims to contextualize the current state of cyber security of the e-banking sector in Bangladesh, including threats, incidents, and mitigation techniques. The research tries to highlight the importance of security guidelines and solutions developed with new threat vectors and localization of Bangladesh in mind. The research shows additional security recommendations including a proposed framework alongside pre-existing tools and methodologies. © 2022 Elsevier B.V., All rights reserved.
