UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85128908122&doi=10.1109%2FICEARS53579.2022.9751853&partnerID=40&md5=b4db82dd90613a4fe03b67329d44bcaa
AD  - Jawaharlal Nehru Technological University Anantapur, Department of Computer Science and Engineering, Anantapur, India
AB  - The development of in-network services in the IT industry influences cloud usage rapidly for storage and distribution. As such new computing and service technology needed data owners to entrust their important data to place in the cloud service provider (CSP), in such case, the concerns surrounding the data security and privacy for the outsourced data is the biggest challenge. But it has become a widespread and apparent trend to utilize cloud services for data outsourcing. However, in consideration of the widespread interest in cloud computing, data security is the primary concern regarding the storage of cloud data is related to reliability and privacy, which are considered to be major difficulties to the acceptance of the cloud. To facilitate these issues and challenges, in this paper a Unique Key Integrity Agreement (UKIA) Approach has been presented. It initially investigates the issues and challenges to provide secure and reliable data storage and access methods in cloud computing. The UKIA proposes a framework to have secure and private data exchange between the data owner and consumer in support of an intermediate domain service provider to offer data storage and privacy access in the cloud. It implements a secure authorization model for the intermediate domain service providers, which will confine the required reliability and privacy assurance for the data owners for data storage, and a UKIA method for secure and private data access. The experiment analysis performs an asymptotic complexity comparison of the results to indicate the security goals with the practical efficiency of the computation in the security and privacy needs. © 2022 Elsevier B.V., All rights reserved.
