EP  - 1015
DO  - 10.1007/978-3-030-30577-2_89
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85075319079&doi=10.1007%2F978-3-030-30577-2_89&partnerID=40&md5=35f7633b683e242cbf010b3853e798c8
AD  - Pune Institute of Computer Technology, Pune, India
AD  - Indian Institute of Technology Madras, Chennai, India
AB  - Advances in Information Technology field have replaced local storage of data with cloud storage which has become the crux of the day. As the number of people adopting storage as a service is increasing, security of personal and private information stored on cloud remains a major concern. Different approaches to achieve security have been used like encryption, file division, access control security (ACS), authentication, authorization and many more. We demonstrate ‘Enigma’: A hybrid approach to file security in cloud that outdoes all these traditional methods and provides unmatched security. In our approach, we introduce the concept of ‘jigsaw division’ which not only enhances division approach but also allows us to employ selective encryption thus combining best of both worlds. We show that our strategy provides better security and at the same time minimally hampers the retrieval time. We model our system using graph and demonstrate the use of vertex coloring algorithm for distributing file fragments across different nodes. By doing this we aim to turn the act of hacking and retrieving meaningful information into a puzzle for all the Black Hats out there!. © 2019 Elsevier B.V., All rights reserved.
