AD  - Altiux Innovations Inc, Mountain View, United States
AD  - Yakın Doğu Üniversitesi, Department of Software Engineering, Nicosia, Cyprus
AB  - The Internet of Things (IoT) is a novel paradigm where entities become part of the Internet. It has converged technologies in terms of sensing, computing, information processing, networking and controlling intelligent technologies. Among the technologies converged, we can count wireless sensor networks (WSNs), intelligent sensing, remote sensing, low-energy wireless communications and cloud computing. The distributed sensor networks are used as interconnecting devices to transmit measured information, and control instructions. Therefore, energy efficiency and performability of WSNs are critical for IoT applications. WSNs are composed of base stations and numerous low-cost mobility nodes which have restricted resources, in terms of communication, storage and computation facilities. For more than 15 years, especially in the last decade, different approaches have been proposed and designed for considering the collaborative nature of WSNs. Since each mobility node has its own sensing unit, data-processing unit, a module for short-range communication and a power-supply unit, the energy limitations, and potential solutions for these restrictions are investigated heavily in the existing literature. Various improvements are introduced from physical layer to energy-efficient medium access control procedures, and a wealth of studies are performed for effective duty cycles as well as routing and clustering algorithms. For all these new approaches and novel techniques, the performance evaluation in terms of energy efficiency, coverage, performance and availability is essential. In general, we can classify the methods employed for evaluation under three categories as implementation of real test beds, computer simulations and analytical modelling. Among these evaluation methods, simulation studies are particularly popular because of its low cost in terms of resources (efforts and time) as compared to the real test beds and provide flexibility compared to existing analytical solution approaches. The existing simulation tools are quite capable when traditional networks, cellular systems and ad hoc communications are considered; however, since the WSN infrastructures are heavily dependent on the type of application they are being used for, the existing simulators have significant drawbacks. As an example, when performance, availability and/or energy evaluation of sensor-based systems are considered, the separation of software architecture, hardware configuration, characteristics of the environment and deployment strategies related concerns are usually not considered. Since scalability and cost efficiency of IoT services are directly related with optimum use of information provider sensor nodes, in this study simulation tools are investigated and analysed in detail. The main aim of this study is to specify an approach which considers collaborative nature of WSNs, correlation characteristics, all potential entities from physical up to application layer, hardware configuration as well as the characteristics of the environment of deployment. It is also desirable to introduce separation of concerns to reuse the models developed across projects and organisations while realistic lifetime estimations and performance evaluations are provided. © 2020 Elsevier B.V., All rights reserved.
