AD  - Department of Computer Science, West Lafayette, United States
AB  - In service-oriented architecture, services can communicate and share data amongst themselves. It is necessary to provide role-based access control for data. In addition, data leakages made by authorized insiders to unauthorized services should be detected and reported back to the data owner. In this paper, we propose a solution that uses role-and attribute-based access control for data exchange among services, including services hosted by untrusted environments. Our approach provides data leakage prevention and detection for multiple leakage scenarios. We also propose a damage assessment model for data leakages. The implemented prototype supports a privacy-preserving exchange of Electronic Health Records that can be hosted by untrusted cloud providers, as well as detecting leakages made by insiders. © 2019 Elsevier B.V., All rights reserved.
