TI  - DetLogic: A black-box approach for detecting logic vulnerabilities in web applications
PY  - 2018
T2  - Journal of Network and Computer Applications
VL  - 109
SP  - 89
EP  - 109
DO  - 10.1016/j.jnca.2018.01.008
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85044864053&doi=10.1016%2Fj.jnca.2018.01.008&partnerID=40&md5=b82f804b5d1d7df83b454eef0ae31c16
AD  - National Institute of Technology Karnataka, Department of Computer Science and Engineering, Mangalore, India
AB  - Web applications are subject to attacks by malicious users owing to the fact that the applications are implemented by software developers with insufficient knowledge about secure programming. The implementation flaws arising due to insecure coding practices allow attackers to exploit the application in order to perform adverse actions leading to undesirable consequences. These flaws can be categorized into injection and logic flaws. As large number of tools and solutions are available for addressing injection flaws, the focus of the attackers is shifting towards exploitation of logic flaws. The logic flaws allow attackers to compromise the application-specific functionality against the expectations of the stakeholders, and hence it is important to identify these flaws in order to avoid exploitation. Therefore, a prototype called DetLogic is developed for detecting different types of logic vulnerabilities such as parameter manipulation, access-control, and workflow bypass vulnerabilities in web applications. DetLogic employs black-box approach, and models the intended behavior of the application as an annotated finite state machine, which is subsequently used for deriving constraints related to input parameters, access-control, and workflows. The derived constraints are violated for simulating attack vectors to identify the vulnerabilities. DetLogic is evaluated against benchmark applications and is found to work effectively. © 2018 Elsevier B.V., All rights reserved.
